            "If practising in England: a 3-month post-call practical course and 1 year of pupillage were required (no fee earned in the first 6 months of pupillage; a pupillage fee of 100 guineas was formerly payable, now abolished).",
            "Barristers' work: court advocacy, drafting court process, giving legal opinions — no direct client contact (briefed by a solicitor). Solicitors: articled to a firm of solicitors in England for 4 years (2 years for a law graduate, with the same enhanced salary benefit).",
            "Passed Solicitors' Part I and Final by the Law Society (a school for solicitors' training courses was established in 1922, attendance compulsory).",
            "Solicitors' work: drawing leases and conveyances, drafting wills, preparing commercial agreements, giving general legal advice, and briefing a barrister when litigation was needed.",
            "Deficiencies of an English-trained lawyer practising in Nigeria: 1. In England he trained and practised as either a barrister or a solicitor; in Nigeria, on enrolment, he practised as both. 2. He studied English textbooks, law reports and the unitary system of government, while Nigeria is a federation with a federal constitution; he had no knowledge of Nigerian customary law or Nigerian land law. 3. Most trained as barristers without a university degree, and did not take the 3-month post-call course or the 1-year pupillage after returning to Nigeria; until 1967 there was no mandatory course of lectures for returning lawyers (only dining was compulsory).",
